.TH SNIP 1 "2026-11-06" "notcoreutils" "notcoreutils Manual"
.SH NAME
snip \- command snippet manager with tags, in the spirit of pet
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snip add
[\fB\-t\fR \fITAGS\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIDESC\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR...
.br
.B snip list\fR|\fBpick\fR|\fBrun\fR|\fBrm
[\fB\-t\fR \fITAG\fR]
.br
.B snip tags
.br
.B snip edit
.br
.B snip init bash\fR|\fBzsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snip
keeps commands you want to find again. Each snippet has a command, an optional description and
optional comma\-separated tags. Choosing happens in
.BR fzf (1)
when one is on PATH, else in a numbered menu on the terminal.
.PP
A command may contain placeholders, \fI<name>\fR or \fI<name=default>\fR. They are asked for on
the terminal when the snippet is picked; the same name is asked once.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add
Store a snippet. The command is the remaining arguments, or stdin when there are none. A command that
is already stored is refused.
.TP
.B list
Print the snippets, only those with the given tag when \fB\-t\fR is used.
.TP
.B pick
Choose a snippet, fill its placeholders and print the command on stdout. This is what the shell key uses.
.TP
.B run
Like \fBpick\fR, then run the command with sh \-c.
.TP
.B rm
Choose a snippet and delete it.
.TP
.B tags
Every tag and how many snippets carry it.
.TP
.B edit
Open the store in $EDITOR (vi when unset).
.TP
.B init bash\fR|\fBzsh
Print shell code: Ctrl\-X s pastes a picked snippet into the command line at the cursor.
Use it with \fBeval "$(snip init bash)"\fR in the shell's rc file. The zsh variant is untested.
.SH TYPING A COMMAND
After \fBeval "$(snip init bash)"\fR press Ctrl\-X s in the middle of a command line: pick, fill, and the text
lands at the cursor. To store the command you just ran, use history expansion:
\fBsnip add \-t git \-d "undo commit" \-\- !!\fR
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $SNIP_FILE
else \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snip/snippets\fR, else \fI~/.config/snip/snippets\fR. One snippet per line:
TAGS, DESCRIPTION and COMMAND separated by tabs; newline, tab and backslash are written as \\n, \\t, \\\\.
Comment lines are not kept.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on failure or a cancelled pick, 2 on a usage error.
